  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0151 database is used for managing a company's assets, departments, and users. It contains 14 tables, each with its own set of columns and relationships.\nThe announcements table stores information about announcements made by users, including the user who made the announcement, the body of the announcement, and any action text and URL associated with the announcement. The table also includes timestamps for when the announcement was created and updated.\nThe assets_departments table is a many-to-many relationship between assets and departments, allowing each asset to be associated with multiple departments and each department to have multiple assets.\nThe password_resets table stores information about password reset requests, including the email address of the user who requested the reset and the token used to verify the request.\nThe categories table stores information about asset categories, including the name of the category and the parent category (if applicable).\nThe invoices table stores information about invoices, including the user who the invoice is for, the amount due, the due date, and the status of the invoice.\nThe assets table stores information about the company's assets, including the name, serial number, manufacturer, category, location, department, and user who is responsible for the asset.\nThe api_tokens table stores information about API tokens, including the user who the token belongs to, the name of the token, the token itself, and the expiration date.\nThe notifications table stores information about notifications, including the user who the notification is for, the message of the notification, and a boolean indicating whether the notification has been read.\nThe tickets table stores information about maintenance tickets, including the asset associated with the ticket, the user who created the ticket, the status of the ticket, and a description of the issue.\nThe maintenance table stores information about maintenance records, including the asset associated with the record, the description of the maintenance, and the date the maintenance was performed.\nThe departments table stores information about the company's departments, including the name of the department and the location of the department.\nThe locations table stores information about the company's locations, including the name of the location and the address of the location.\nThe manufacturers table stores information about the company's manufacturers, including the name of the manufacturer.\nThe users table stores information about the company's users, including the email address, name, password, and remember token for each user.\nOverall, the schemabench_0151 database is designed to provide a comprehensive system for managing a company's assets, departments, and users, with each table serving a specific purpose and containing the necessary information to support the relationships between them.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing an open-source IT asset management system called Snipe-IT. This system is designed to assist organizations in efficiently tracking and visualizing various assets including hardware, software licenses, accessories, and consumables. Built on the Laravel framework, Snipe-IT provides a user-friendly web interface that facilitates asset management tasks.\n\nObjectives\n\nThe primary objectives of Snipe-IT are to enable organizations to:\n- Track and manage IT assets effectively.\n- Visualize asset histories and statuses.\n- Implement efficient asset check-in and check-out processes.\n- Generate and utilize barcodes for asset tracking.\n- Send automated email notifications for asset-related updates.\n- Manage user access through role-based controls.\n- Integrate with LDAP/Active Directory for streamlined user management.\n- Utilize an API for automation and enhanced integration.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\nThe main stakeholders and users of Snipe-IT include:\n- IT teams in businesses, schools, and non-profits.\n- System administrators responsible for asset tracking and management.\n- End-users who interact with and check out assets.\n- Organizational decision-makers interested in analytics and reporting.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n1. Asset Management: \n   - Track hardware, software licenses, accessories, and consumables.\n   - Maintain detailed asset histories including maintenance and ticket records.\n   - Perform asset check-in and check-out operations.\n\n2. Barcode Generation: \n   - Generate barcodes for assets to facilitate physical tracking.\n   \n3. Notifications: \n   - Send automated email alerts for asset updates, upcoming maintenance, and more.\n\n4. Role-Based Access Control: \n   - Implement permissions and access controls based on user roles.\n   \n5. LDAP/Active Directory Integration: \n   - Align user management with organizational directories.\n   \n6. API Access: \n   - Provide API endpoints for external integration and automation.\n\n7. Multi-User and Multi-Location Support: \n   - Enable system use across various user profiles and geographical locations.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Usability: \n   - Ensure a user-friendly web interface that is easy to navigate.\n\n2. Scalability: \n   - Support multiple users and locations without degradation of performance.\n\n3. Flexibility: \n   - Allow for customization and self-hosting to meet diverse organizational needs.\n\n4. Security: \n   - Ensure secure data handling, access control, and integration processes.\n\n5. Reliability: \n   - Maintain consistent performance and availability across installations.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0151.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0151\nCREATE TABLE `announcements` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `body` text NOT NULL,\n  `action_text` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `action_url` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `announcements_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `assets` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `serial_number` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `manufacturer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `location_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`manufacturer_id`) REFERENCES `manufacturers` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`category_id`) REFERENCES `categories` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`location_id`) REFERENCES `locations` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_4` FOREIGN KEY (`department_id`) REFERENCES `departments` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_5` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `assets_departments` (\n  `asset_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`asset_id`, `department_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_departments_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`asset_id`) REFERENCES `assets` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_departments_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`department_id`) REFERENCES `departments` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `categories` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `parent_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `categories_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`parent_id`) REFERENCES `categories` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `departments` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `location_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `departments_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`location_id`) REFERENCES `locations` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `invoices` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `amount_due` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `due_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `invoices_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `locations` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `maintenance` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `asset_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text NOT NULL,\n  `maintenance_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `maintenance_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`asset_id`) REFERENCES `assets` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `manufacturers` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `notifications` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `message` text NOT NULL,\n  `is_read` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `notifications_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `password_resets` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `tickets` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `asset_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `tickets_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`asset_id`) REFERENCES `assets` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `tickets_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `remember_token` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `api_tokens` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `expires_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `api_tokens_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
